          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval combining lexical and semantic search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two independent sources; hybrid retrieval benefits documented in production contexts but broader patterns not yet corroborated.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines **sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) with **dense vector search**, then merges the two ranked result sets. The merged results are typically combined via **reciprocal rank fusion**, which captures both exact term matches and semantic similarity in a single ranked list.\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nIt has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nUse reciprocal rank fusion for robust retrieval when combining ranked result sets from multiple sources.\\n\u0022}"
        }
